(6. Methods, assumptions and sources) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.078 g
42.5196850393700814 452.0524015748031843 Td
(Orbits: CelesTrak GP element sets \(US Space Force catalogue\) propagated with SGP4 \(satellite.js\). Accuracy about 1 km) Tj
T* (at the element epoch, degrading with age. Positions and pass times were cross-checked against the independent Python) Tj
T* (sgp4 package \(agreement < 0.06 deg, pass times within 10 s\) and against Heavens-Above \(same passes; it uses a 10) Tj
T* (deg mask\).) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.078 g
42.5196850393700814 397.9106692913386496 Td
(Contacts: 48 h scan at 20 s with AOS/LOS refined by bisection to under 1 s, above the chosen elevation mask. Target) Tj
T* (opportunities use the elevation equivalent to the maximum off-nadir angle: cos\(el\) = sin\(eta\) \(R + h\) / R. GEO relay line of) Tj
T* (sight: the LEO-GEO segment must clear the Earth by 60 km.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.078 g
42.5196850393700814 355.8870472440945605 Td
(Delivery time = waiting for the next contact \(multi-pass drain for large files, at the nominal rate\) + propagation \(range / c\) +) Tj
T* (serialisation \(payload bits in CCSDS AOS frames of 1,115 bytes, one frame minimum, at the nominal rate\) + terrestrial) Tj
T* (fibre \(great-circle distance x 1.5 route factor at 2/3 c plus 0.5 ms per hop for 12 hops\) + the ground processing time) Tj
T* (entered. GEO relay: LEO-GEO about 37,800 km, GEO-gateway from geometry, relay rate from the catalogue, fibre from) Tj
T* (the gateway.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.078 g
42.5196850393700814 289.6272047244095802 Td
(Link budget: free-space loss, ITU-R P.676 gaseous absorption \(zenith value / sin el\), ITU-R P.838 rain \(k, alpha at the) Tj
T* (band; 3 km rain height; P.618 reduction\), pointing 0.5 dB, polarisation 0.3 dB, implementation 1.5 dB, antenna efficiency) Tj
T* (0.6, roll-off 0.35, required Eb/N0 from CCSDS 130.1-G performance curves. Uncoded BER = 0.5 erfc\(sqrt\(Eb/N0\)\); coded) Tj
T* (BER is a waterfall model \(1e-6 at the requirement, one decade per 0.3 dB, floor 1e-12\), indicative only.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.078 g
42.5196850393700814 235.4854724409449318 Td
(Why real missions quote hours \(docs/LATENCY_RESEARCH.md\): a satellite has about 10 minutes of ground contact per) Tj
T* (100-minute orbit and 4-6 windows a day with any one station; it collects 0.5-1.5 TB/day into its recorder, so imagery) Tj
T* (queues for hours behind earlier data and the X-band transmitter is duty-cycle limited \(Sentinel-1: 30 min per orbit\); then) Tj
T* (the product is processed under a timeliness policy \(Sentinel NRT 1-3 h, standard 24 h; Landsat ~5 h\). The simulator) Tj
T* (models the queue \(backlog, FIFO or priority\), the duty cap and the processing time; a semantic message avoids the) Tj
T* (queue and the payload transmitter entirely on the TT&C route.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.078 g
42.5196850393700814 157.1075196850393922 Td
(What is NOT modelled: station scheduling and contention, encryption and product processing, data policy \(e.g. ESA's) Tj
T* (near-real-time commitment for Sentinel products is under 3 h, set by processing and policy rather than the radio\), antenna) Tj
T* (slew and acquisition time \(typically 1 to 2 minutes at the start of a pass\), and the actual availability of relay service.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0.078 g
42.5196850393700814 115.0838976377953031 Td
(Confidence tags in the catalogue: SOURCED = operator or engineering source \(listed on the card and in section 1\);) Tj
T* (TYPICAL = secondary source \(eoPortal, trade press\), treat as +/-20 %; ASSUMED = class default. Satellite EIRP and the) Tj
T* (ground G/T are never published per mission and are always assumptions, so the margin and BER are indicative rather) Tj
T* (than a certified budget.) Tj
ET
